---
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const facts = [
  { k: 'City', v: 'Oakland' },
  { k: 'Year', v: '2000' },
  { k: 'Original', v: '1917 market hall' },
  { k: 'Program', v: 'Cohousing + affordable rentals + shops' },
  { k: 'Developer', v: 'EBALDC' },
  { k: 'Transit', v: '2 blocks from BART' },
];

const gallery = [
  {
    placeholder: 'Courtyard — photo needed',
    cap: 'The courtyard carved from the market hall — 1917 roof trusses kept overhead',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Oakland%2010th%20Street%20Market-7.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Almonroth · CC BY-SA 3.0',
    creditHref: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oakland_10th_Street_Market-7.jpg',
  },
  { placeholder: 'Cohousing common house — photo needed', cap: 'The cohousing common room on the court' },
  {
    placeholder: 'Washington St front — photo needed',
    cap: 'Shops and restaurants keep the 1917 street face',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Oakland%2010th%20Street%20Market-6.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Almonroth · CC BY-SA 3.0',
    creditHref: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oakland_10th_Street_Market-6.jpg',
  },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Swan's Market"
  subtitle="The closest local cousin — courtyard life two BART stops away."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/10th%20Street%20Market%2C%20Oakland%2C%20CA%2C%20at%20sunset.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Swan's Market hero — courtyard in the market hall (photo needed)"
  heroCredit="Dreamyshade · CC BY-SA 4.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:10th_Street_Market,_Oakland,_CA,_at_sunset.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/village-homes/', label: 'Village Homes' }}
  next={{ href: '/vauban/', label: 'Vauban' }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      A 1917 market hall in Old Oakland, rebuilt around a courtyard: a 20-unit cohousing community,
      affordable rental apartments, restaurants, offices, and cultural space under one historic
      roof. It is two blocks from BART and about fifteen minutes from Berkeley — the nearest built
      answer to this site's whole argument, even if it stays low-rise.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      What to steal: the coalition. A community development corporation (EBALDC) held the project
      together, so cohousing, affordable housing, and commerce could share one roof and one loan —
      the finance box American lenders said doesn't exist.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>
      It already works here: courtyard + cohousing + shops + affordability, one BART ride from
      Berkeley.
    </li>
    <li>A CDC as developer is the American translation of the Baugruppe — mission absorbs the margin.</li>
    <li>Mixed program under one historic roof financed what no single use could.</li>
    <li>The missing ingredient is only height — Swan's at 6 stories is the Berkeley brief.</li>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources">
    Sources: <a href="https://ebaldc.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ebaldc.org</a> and
    published project histories.
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
